---
import ComponentDoc from '../../layouts/ComponentDoc.astro';
import Section from '@esa/docs/Section.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/ApiTable.astro';

const describe = {
  src: 'URL of a GeoJSON FeatureCollection. <strong>Fetched by this component</strong>, not handed to the engine as a URL &mdash; see Why the data is fetched here.',
  data: 'An already-parsed FeatureCollection. Wins over <code>src</code>. Set as a JS property.',
  label: 'REQUIRED. Names the layer for assistive tech &mdash; it becomes the accessible name of the mirror list. Without it the mirror is an unlabelled pile of buttons.',
  'name-property': 'Which feature property holds the human-readable name, used for the mirror button labels.',
  hoverable: 'Highlight the feature under the pointer, using engine <code>feature-state</code> rather than re-rendering the layer.',
  selectable: 'Emit <code>featureselect</code> on click, and expose the keyboard mirror.',
  'fit-bounds': 'Fit the camera to the data once it has loaded.',
};

const events = [
  {
    name: 'featureselect',
    type: '{ features, feature, lngLat, index, count }',
    description:
      'A feature was chosen, by pointer or by keyboard. <code>features</code> is every distinct feature under the point, topmost first; <code>feature</code> is the one at <code>index</code>. All plain GeoJSON &mdash; no engine objects cross this boundary.',
  },
];
---
<ComponentDoc name="Map GeoJSON Layer" category="Maps" slug="esa-map-geojson" type="wc" status="stable"
  summary={'Renders polygons, lines and points from GeoJSON inside <a href="/components/esa-map">esa-map</a> &mdash; theme-aware monochrome fill and a hairline outline with no styling required. Emits selections as plain GeoJSON, and mirrors every feature into a real focusable list so shapes drawn by WebGL are reachable without sight.'}>

  <Section id="preview" title="Preview">
    <p>
      The live example is on the <a href="/components/esa-map#preview">Map page</a> &mdash; all 58
      California counties, hoverable and selectable, with the selection driving a popup. It lives
      there because a layer is meaningless without a host to mount into.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="a11y" title="The accessibility mirror is the point of this component">
    <p>
      A shape drawn by WebGL <strong>is a pixel</strong>. It has no role, no name, no focus and no
      presence in the accessibility tree. Nothing reaches it: not a token, not a
      <code>@media</code> block, not a screen reader. And <strong>axe reports nothing</strong>,
      because there is no markup to be wrong &mdash; so a map can pass every automated check while
      being entirely unavailable to anyone not using a pointer and a working pair of eyes.
    </p>
    <p>
      So every feature also gets a real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> in a visually-hidden list inside
      this component. Same selection path, same <code>featureselect</code> event, same highlight on
      the map. It is not a fallback bolted alongside the map &mdash; it is the map&rsquo;s only
      non-visual representation.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Visually hidden, not hidden.</strong> <code>display: none</code> and
        <code>visibility: hidden</code> both remove an element from the accessibility tree, which
        would delete the very thing this exists for. The clip-rect idiom keeps it focusable and
        readable while taking no space.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deliberately not revealed on focus.</strong> Unlike a skip link, this duplicates
        what is already on screen; flashing 58 county names over the map when someone tabs in would
        be noise. The selection it triggers is visible on the map itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Only when <code>selectable</code>.</strong> A decorative backdrop owes no keyboard
        path to shapes nobody can act on.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section id="fetch" title="Why the data is fetched here">
    <p>
      The obvious implementation hands the engine a URL and lets it load the file. This component
      fetches it instead, and that is load-bearing:
      <code>querySourceFeatures</code> only ever returns what is currently tiled and in view, so a
      mirror built from the engine&rsquo;s own view of the data would <strong>silently omit every
      feature off-screen</strong>. One fetch gives the complete list, which is the only way the
      mirror can be honest.
    </p>
    <p>
      The source is created with <code>generateId</code>. Feature state keys off the feature id and
      the engine only reliably accepts integers there &mdash; a string id, which is exactly what a
      hand-made file tends to carry (a county <em>name</em>, say), silently fails to highlight.
      Generated ids are integers by construction; the human name still travels in
      <code>properties</code>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="styling" title="Monochrome by default, on purpose">
    <p>
      Fill and outline resolve from tier-2 roles through the JS theme bridge, and re-resolve when
      the theme moves. The default is monochrome because <strong>the data is the subject</strong>:
      a categorical palette applied to boundaries invents a meaning the data does not carry.
      Colour by attribute is a deliberate act, not a default.
    </p>
    <p>
      State is carried by fill <em>opacity</em> (7% → 16% hovered → 28% selected) rather than hue,
      since a monochrome layer that changes colour on hover stops being monochrome. The outline
      is never omitted &mdash; in forced-colors it is the only thing separating adjacent shapes
      once the palette collapses to the user&rsquo;s own pair.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three layers are always added &mdash; fill for polygons, line for LineStrings <em>and</em>
      polygon outlines, circle for points &mdash; so one component handles any geometry the file
      contains. Inspecting the data and adding only what is needed breaks the moment a
      FeatureCollection is mixed, which real GIS exports routinely are.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="api" title="API">
    <ApiTable slug="esa-map-geojson" describe={describe} events={events} />
    <p class="meta">
      Clicks are deduped by feature id before being reported. All three layers draw from one
      source, so a click near a boundary returns the same polygon twice &mdash; once from the fill
      and once from its outline &mdash; and reporting &ldquo;2 of 2&rdquo; for one feature would be
      a lie about how many are actually there.
    </p>
  </Section>
</ComponentDoc>

<style>
  .meta { color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464); max-width: 72ch; }
</style>
